1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Today, we're at Bletchley Park in honor of Alan Turing's 100th birthday.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,900
 Bletchley Park, just north of London, in between Cambridge and Oxford, was considered by many

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:12,720
 as the birthplace of modern computer science.

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:20,720
 In 1938, again, anticipation of war, the government code and cipher scroll, and in the main, their

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:25,320
 operational director, a gentleman called Alastair Dennison, had drawn up a list.

6
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:31,120
 He had sent two people out to troll through the people at Oxford, and primarily Cambridge,

7
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,180
 and to some extent Oxford, and drew up a list of people who they approached, who agreed

8
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:39,760
 in the event of war, would report immediately to Bletchley Park.

9
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,800
 Britain declared war on September 3rd, 1939, the following day, September 4th, Turing and

10
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,600
 several others reported to Bletchley Park.

11
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:53,480
 As the requirement for more people was needed, some, predominantly a gentleman called Gordon

12
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,120
 Welchman, who had been on the initial list, he'd arrived the same day as Turing.

13
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:03,200
 He went back to Cambridge, and he started recruiting all his best students, and in effect,

14
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:07,560
 what they created here was the World's First Skunk Works, a secret innovative organization

15
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:09,640
 where there were no rules.

16
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:14,000
 Eventually, their main benefactor became the Prime Minister, Winston Churchill, and they

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 persuaded Churchill that this place needed more resources.

18
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,240
 Churchill agreed, and he wrote a famous ... A letter was written by Welchman, Turing, and

19
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:29,280
 the two deputies to Churchill, and one other number, a gentleman called Stuart Millner-Berry

20
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:31,840
 actually delivered it in person to Downing Street.

21
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,500
 Churchill, amazingly, read the letter the same day it was delivered.

22
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:39,520
 He put a famous "Action This Day" stamp on it, with a handwritten note to his Chief of

23
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:44,600
 Staff, a chap called General Lismay, which said, "Expedite with extreme priority and

24
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,680
 report to me when it is done," and really from that point, September 1941, Bletchley

25
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:51,240
 Park got all the resources they needed.

26
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:56,000
 They threw, in effect, the smartest people together in Britain, and said, "Here's the

27
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 budget.

28
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,440
 This is the end game," and that's why they invented some of these technologies that probably

29
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:03,340
 wouldn't have been invented for years.

30
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:07,760
 In many ways, Bletchley Park was an early version of a multidisciplinary science center,

31
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:11,480
 much like CERN or NCSA is today.

32
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:15,520
 Many brilliant people with different skills and backgrounds were brought together to solve

33
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:17,680
 difficult problems.

34
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,300
 The combination of the skills and collaborative environment resulted not in just solving the

35
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:27,120
 problems of cryptography that they were facing, but in addition, solved broader problems for

36
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:29,360
 all of computing and all of society.

37
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:35,240
 When the people arrived here, they knew when they arrived that the Germans had taken a

38
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:39,800
 machine, a machine which we tend to call Enigma.

39
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,080
 Enigma was a particular variant of an encryption machine.

40
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:48,680
 That machine was modified from the commercial version, and it allowed them to encrypt messages.

41
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:52,680
 This was going to become a machine to be used for operational communications, very short

42
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:55,640
 200, 250-character messages.

43
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:00,620
 Hitler had conceived, and as generals, a type of warfare never seen before, which became

44
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:01,980
 known as Blitzkrieg.

45
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:07,200
 Very fast movement, 50 miles a day, particularly when they invaded France and the latter stages

46
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:08,760
 of their invasion of Poland.

47
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,600
 You couldn't use fixed-line communications.

48
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:12,700
 You needed to use wireless communications.

49
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:17,200
 Here was a device which was portable, weighed 25 pounds, ran off a battery.

50
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,040
 You could encrypt messages, and then as a separate process, they could be sent using

51
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:23,840
 the fairly new technology of wireless radio.

52
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:28,320
 It was the Poles who were the first to recognize that the age of machine cryptography had arrived,

53
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:32,680
 and the sort of people who would be good at dealing with it were mathematicians.

54
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:36,880
 In fact, they even went so far as to put on a course in cryptography at the University

55
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:42,280
 of Poznan, invited 20 or 30 young German-speaking mathematicians to enroll.

56
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:45,640
 It was a very difficult course, and by the time the course was finished, there were really

57
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:47,880
 only three graduates who they recruited.

58
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:53,340
 Their names were Maryan Ryevsky, Hendrik Zagalsky, and Yezi Orozhitsky, and these three were

59
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:58,200
 the core of this team, and they are the ones who really made the early breakthroughs.

60
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,800
 They convened a conference at their secret headquarters.

61
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:05,320
 This was actually July '39, and at that conference, they gave the British everything.

62
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,260
 They revealed what they had done.

63
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:11,320
 They gave them a replica Enigma machine and all of their work.

64
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:15,200
 That information came back to Bletchley Park, and once they were established in Bletchley

65
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:20,120
 Park, they then used the Polish method to break the Enigma machine.

66
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:24,480
 The Poles had actually built some machines themselves, one of which was called a Bomba.

67
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:30,160
 That machine, the name apparently comes from a Polish ice cream dessert of the same name.

68
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:34,160
 It's in effect vanilla ice cream with chocolate sauce around it.

69
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:40,080
 This machine was called Bomb in honor of what the Poles did, but the Poles' technique was

70
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:44,640
 based on the particular way that, at that time, the Germans enciphered their message

71
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:45,640
 headers.

72
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:51,480
 They, at that point, were repeating the message header, and that was the attack which the

73
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:52,960
 Poles developed on it.

74
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:58,480
 They believed, correctly, that when the war would start, that the Germans would change

75
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:01,640
 the way they were doing that, and they called for help.

76
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:07,080
 They told Turing and the people here everything they'd done, which we are and should be very

77
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:12,440
 grateful, but they also proved that it was possible, and that, I think, was the spur

78
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:16,720
 that kicked the Brits into actually doing something about it.

79
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:22,480
 Now, Turing understood the weaknesses of the way they'd done it, and developed a crib-based

80
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:28,640
 mechanism based on the fact that every military organization in the world cannot stop itself

81
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:30,840
 sending stereotyped messages.

82
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:37,960
 He then talked to a brilliant team of engineers, led by Doc Keane, who told him roughly the

83
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:44,440
 speed that it would be possible to examine potential stops for.

84
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:49,000
 Between them, they then designed the machine called Bomb, which would look for that and

85
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,400
 do so in a sensible time.

86
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:56,040
 Here is an example where we have intercepted this message SNMKG.

87
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:01,920
 We're pretty sure that that is, because this particular operator always did this, this

88
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:03,680
 is his morning weather forecast.

89
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:09,880
 So here we have the guest German plain text, "Wetter für Herr Zarger," "Weather forecast."

90
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:10,880
 And the two are lined up.

91
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:17,200
 At that point, the enigmas' ability or inability to encode a letter as itself is useful, because

92
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,240
 you can make sure you've got no clashes here.

93
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:28,480
 And from these guest letters, the crib makers would derive a picture like this called a

94
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:35,800
 menu where you'll notice that at position ZZE here, G is encoded as E, so we have G

95
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:42,280
 going to E. At ZZP at the end here, E goes to V. This is reversible, of course.

96
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:43,920
 We can describe this the other way up.

97
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:55,560
 So here's E to V, that's ZZG here, S is going to V, so here's another link.

98
00:06:55,560 --> 00:07:00,840
 And eventually we can go all the way around and we can close this loop like that.

99
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:03,080
 It was the closed loops that they were looking for.

100
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:09,080
 The operation of the Bomb was simply that if there is a letter which you can feed in

101
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:14,240
 at this point and which comes around through a string of one, two, three, four, five, six

102
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:19,840
 enigmas and comes back as that same letter, then it's possible a position of these wheels

103
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:25,360
 match the position of the wheels when Gunther started encoding his message on his enigma.

104
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:33,280
 It turned out to be a bit more picky about loops and menus than perhaps they had hoped,

105
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:39,880
 although it worked, and at that point Welchman added a completely left field master stroke

106
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:44,920
 called the diagonal board, which I think it is fair to say made the difference between

107
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:47,760
 success and failure to Turing's original idea.

108
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:49,880
 And here are my three diagonal boards.

109
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:51,760
 That's one, that's the other.

110
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:56,560
 This is the third one, kind of any place where you can't see it, but can you see Z down to

111
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,440
 A, okay?

112
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:02,560
 There's nothing connecting that to the machine, it's just bolted there for convenience.

113
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:09,280
 The only connections to the machine are when you connect X into one of the enigmas.

114
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:15,560
 What will happen is when we turn the machine on, you'll see it will run up to speed, okay?

115
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:19,760
 The motor will be running and the clutch will drop in, then you'll see the various carry

116
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:26,840
 mechanisms happening, and then when we stop the machine, you'll see it slow down and then

117
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:27,840
 the clutch drops out.

118
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:34,840
 So, Tony, if you'd run it up for us please.

119
00:08:34,840 --> 00:09:02,320
 Would you turn on carry home?

120
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:07,000
 Okay, thank you.

121
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,200
 So what you saw there was the middle carry, then we switched on carry home to make the

122
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:18,560
 slow carry work all the time, so these bars are just pushing around all the drums.

123
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:23,440
 And you can see that all the 36 enigmas are just in step, they're all just turning and

124
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,600
 they are a resource.

125
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:30,880
 These are the very fast relays, the three banks, actually four, for the chains that

126
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:33,800
 do the detection here.

127
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:41,760
 This is the control logic, and then this is the 26 bit register that notes a stop and

128
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:45,360
 then allows the machine to slow down before it stops.

129
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:50,200
 Our favorite statistic is the sheer amount of wire in the thing.

130
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:57,560
 We've lost count, but it's somewhere between 10 and 12 miles of wire in each one of these.

131
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:02,160
 Then there was two main systems they were using, Enigma was their operational system,

132
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:06,680
 that was being used for specific operational communications, again, very short, maximum

133
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:12,560
 of 250 characters, instructions, orders for a Panzer Division to move from one location

134
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:18,040
 to another, orders for a submarine to attack a convoy, for aircraft during the Battle of

135
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:21,600
 Britain to attack specific targets.

136
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:27,900
 The Germans started to introduce, through probably 1941, a different system.

137
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:32,640
 The intercept service, which Britain had put into place to intercept these messages, started

138
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:37,960
 hearing traffic, which was clearly not enigma traffic, enigma traffic with Morse code, easily

139
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:39,960
 identifiable.

140
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:45,040
 People can be trained fairly simply to transcribe, to listen to Morse code and transcribe the

141
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,040
 letters.

142
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:47,040
 This was a totally different signal.

143
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,800
 It was not discernible, it was not transcribable.

144
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:55,840
 This was Hitler's communication channel to his army general, so very long-winded communications,

145
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:57,640
 top secret.

146
00:10:57,640 --> 00:11:04,120
 This was an even more daunting task, arguably, than Enigma, because this encryption machine

147
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:08,840
 that was used with this system, there were various ones of them, Hitler referred to these

148
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:15,640
 as his Geheimschreiber's, his secret writers, several companies, Siemens, that exists today,

149
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:19,160
 were making a machine, Haglund, but there was also the Lorenz Company, and that was

150
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:23,600
 the primary machine, particularly Lorenz at SN42.

151
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:28,920
 That machine, and unlike Enigma that had three encryption wheels, had 12 encryption wheels.

152
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:33,800
 They approached the post office research lab at Dulles Hill in London, a young engineer

153
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:36,800
 called Tommy Flowers went into the project.

154
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:39,520
 He was in effect told, "Well, go ahead, if you can get approval."

155
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:44,120
 Flowers went back to the post office, got approval, put a small core team together,

156
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:49,480
 and in less than a year had a working prototype, quite a remarkable achievement.

157
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:55,820
 The first Colossus as this machine became known, the Mark I, had 1,500 valves or vacuum

158
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:56,820
 tubes in it.

159
00:11:56,820 --> 00:11:59,560
 They brought it to Bletchley Park, it worked almost straight away.

160
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:04,400
 The people here were convinced, they asked for a more powerful computer, Flowers already

161
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:10,160
 had anticipated this, he already had it in production, 2,500 valves in an era when no

162
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:16,040
 machine that used valves had more than about 15, here was a machine with 2,500.

163
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:20,520
 Flowers was told that they needed the Mark II by June 1, 1944, because there was a certain

164
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:27,280
 date in the diary, June 5, 1944 was a scheduled day for Operation Overlord D-Day, it actually

165
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:30,200
 ended up being June 6 because of bad weather.

166
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:34,560
 Flowers delivered the first Mark II to Bletchley Park on June 1, they switched it on, it worked

167
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:35,560
 straight away.

168
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:39,480
 They immediately started decrypting messages between the German High Command, and much

169
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:43,880
 to their delight, they discovered that all of the subterfuge about the D-Day landings

170
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:44,880
 had worked.

171
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:48,720
 Hitler had believed that the main invasion was going to be at the Port of Calais, not

172
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:49,720
 at Normandy.

173
00:12:49,720 --> 00:12:55,360
 That intelligence was fed back to General Eisenhower at Allied Supreme Command headquarters,

174
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:59,720
 and there is reasonable evidence to conclude that D-Day might well not have gone ahead

175
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:04,400
 on June 6, 1944 without the intelligence from Bletchley Park.

176
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:11,940
 This is the Colossus computer at Bletchley Park, now we're in Hut 4 at Bletchley Park,

177
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:16,560
 which was one of the first purpose-built computer centers, and some 10 of these machines were

178
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:20,240
 installed here starting in January 1944.

179
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:27,440
 Now, in the years previously, a new way of decrypting messages was discovered that was

180
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:32,200
 being used in Germany, and it was those high-level messages between Hitler and his generals,

181
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:37,880
 and they were encrypted on a machine called a Lorenz machine, and it used teleprinter

182
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:40,720
 codes to actually transmit those messages.

183
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:49,280
 Now, by '43, it was taking some six weeks to actually decode those messages, and decode

184
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,280
 those messages laboriously by hand.

185
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:57,880
 The problem with that is, within six weeks, the usefulness of the intelligence that you

186
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:02,080
 gain from those messages is obviously gone, so that process needed to be speeded up.

187
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:07,440
 Now, some techniques have been tried, some of the electronic techniques have been tried

188
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:15,160
 here at Bletchley Park, but this machine, when it was put into use in early '44, reduced

189
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:20,320
 that six-week period to decode the message down to six hours, and that's really just

190
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:23,640
 a phenomenal jump.

191
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:28,920
 The messages themselves were received at various intercept stations around the country and

192
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:30,400
 were punched onto paper tape.

193
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:35,240
 Now, there were two paper tapes on the machine here at the moment, one that we're running

194
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:38,560
 and one which is ready for the next run.

195
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:45,520
 Now, this tape, there are two here, if I take one, each row across the tape are punched

196
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:49,640
 by a pole, and each of those is an alphanumeric character.

197
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:54,960
 And that's the encrypted message that was received as being punched onto tape.

198
00:14:54,960 --> 00:15:03,240
 Now, although Colossus has really all the elements of a modern electronic computer,

199
00:15:03,240 --> 00:15:08,400
 it doesn't have the memory that we expect in a stored program computer.

200
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:14,200
 So what happens is that tape of that message, some 5,000 characters of that message is actually

201
00:15:14,200 --> 00:15:21,360
 formed in a loop, and is read over here with a series of photoelectric sensors and lights,

202
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:28,760
 and each character is read, and the 5,000 characters a second are read.

203
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:34,960
 Now, each trunk of the machine we can hear there is another 5,000 characters being read

204
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:36,560
 into the machine.

205
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:41,360
 So we're reading that same 5,000 characters each time.

206
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:45,400
 This is the control panel of the Colossus computer.

207
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:48,800
 So it's this side of the machine that we would actually be, that the Wrens and the Wartime

208
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:49,800
 would actually be using.

209
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:56,080
 Now, I can set up the particular algorithm I want to test and routines I want to test

210
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:58,520
 using these control panels.

211
00:15:58,520 --> 00:16:02,880
 And each clunk of the machine at the moment is another state where we're actually trying

212
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:08,480
 a new algorithm and repeating that algorithm on that repeating 5,000 characters.

213
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:13,480
 The results that the machine detects are presented here.

214
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:20,560
 Now, we're doing a statistical analysis of that encrypted tape.

215
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:26,480
 Now, the machine would run for some 6 hours.

216
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:31,820
 There are 2,500 valves in this machine.

217
00:16:31,820 --> 00:16:37,100
 You can't see that many from the front, but once we go between the act of the machines,

218
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:39,000
 you get an idea of the scale.

219
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,360
 2,500 valves.

220
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:47,560
 Each valve has a heater, a hot wire heater in the center of the valve, and operates at

221
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:51,520
 about 2,300 volts DC.

222
00:16:51,520 --> 00:17:03,080
 Now, 2,500 valves plus the power supplies generates over 8 kilowatts of electricity.

223
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:06,720
 And by this point, when we're standing in between the racks, you can feel the heat coming

224
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:14,680
 off of these racks.

225
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:21,920
 Colossus is a combination of electronics, and we can see the electronics here in these

226
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:29,580
 valve chassis members, and also electromechanical switch gear.

227
00:17:29,580 --> 00:17:32,680
 So there are backs of relay panels here.

228
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:38,520
 These big single motion detectors are a sort of motorized switch that will actually spin

229
00:17:38,520 --> 00:17:39,520
 around.

230
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:41,280
 There's banks of those in the machine.

231
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:47,600
 The technology is equipment that was well known for the British Post Office.

232
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:52,880
 This machine was designed by a chap called Tommy Flowers, and Tommy Flowers was an engineer

233
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:57,840
 at the research center in London for the British Post Office.

234
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:06,740
 Tommy was asked to look at the problem of how to automate this task, this manual task.

235
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:10,800
 And Tommy came up with the idea of using electronics to do this.

236
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:17,640
 At the time, the idea of using more than half a dozen valves in any circuit was just simply

237
00:18:17,640 --> 00:18:19,440
 poo poo.

238
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:20,960
 Valves had a bad reputation.

239
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:22,720
 People had valves in their radios at home.

240
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:24,720
 They failed when they switched the radios on.

241
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:28,800
 So the idea of using two and a half thousand was just simply phenomenal.

242
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:35,120
 But Tommy understood that it's probably-- it's almost certainly the thermal shock that kills

243
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:36,120
 valves in the first place.

244
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:41,360
 So if you leave the machine on and don't subject it to that shock, you won't have a problem.

245
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:45,840
 Now all the 10 colossus machines here at Bletchley Park were left on permanently.

246
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:52,280
 The machines were operated by Rens in three shifts throughout the day.

247
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:56,040
 We have to be equally careful.

248
00:18:56,040 --> 00:19:00,280
 This is obviously-- this is a replica of the machine itself.

249
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:02,240
 But the same problems apply.

250
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:09,520
 We need to be careful in bringing the supply voltages up gradually so we don't suffer from

251
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:15,120
 thermal shock and in the same way shutting the machine down.

252
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:21,440
 Tommy had the idea of generating the key, because it's a key that we're comparing with

253
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:25,120
 that encrypted tape, generating that key electronically.

254
00:19:25,120 --> 00:19:30,520
 And these counters here, they're called the thyrotron rings, the thyrotron valves, actually

255
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:33,800
 hold that count.

256
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:36,880
 Now we describe colossus as not having a memory.

257
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:44,760
 We say not having a memory in the sense that modern computers have a memory that's common

258
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:46,560
 for data and for programs.

259
00:19:46,560 --> 00:19:50,320
 Well, the program here is set on those control panels at the front.

260
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:57,800
 And that's switch and plug programmed, much like, say, its contemporary ENAG machine.

261
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:02,880
 But these machines, these valves at the back, do act as a store in the sense of counters.

262
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:06,600
 And they're actually counting the score that we're actually getting, each passed through

263
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:11,160
 the algorithm.

264
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:18,720
 We can see at the back here, well, certainly some of the modern technology, I suspect the

265
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:23,560
 wartime guys that were building and debugging these machines, would have given anything

266
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:28,360
 for a reliable oscilloscope and let alone a logic analyzer.

267
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:34,520
 But if I look into the machine down here, back, you can see the projected image that's

268
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:40,720
 coming off the tape, and that's shining onto five photosensitive valves.

269
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:45,560
 And it's those valves that are actually reading the 5,000 characters every second.

270
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:49,240
 And paper tape was used well into the 1960s and '70s.

271
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:53,920
 At this speed, it was simply phenomenal, and hadn't really been thought of before.

272
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:59,600
 And our original museum director, Tony Versailles, wanted to rebuild the Colossus and build a

273
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:03,240
 replica of the Colossus machine, and this was some 20 years ago.

274
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:05,360
 There's very little information out there.

275
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:12,480
 There are a few pictures that were kept, possibly illegally, a few scraps of circuit diagrams,

276
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:16,640
 and just the memories of some of the original pioneers who worked on the machine.

277
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:21,760
 And when Tony started the plant, Tommy Flowers was still alive and remembered and was able

278
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:25,640
 to draw circuits, because finally the parts as well.

279
00:21:25,640 --> 00:21:29,160
 But the important thing is the machine was designed by a post office engineer at the

280
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:34,360
 time, an engineer that was used to designing systems for telephone switching.

281
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:40,280
 So a lot of the components here, everything from the relay banks to the switches to the

282
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:47,800
 power supplies, were common to British post offices in the pre-war period.

283
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:54,440
 So when Tony then wants to find the components to rebuild the replica, he's lucky in the

284
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:59,960
 sense that the last of those exchanges are being decommissioned from post offices around

285
00:21:59,960 --> 00:22:00,960
 the country.

286
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:05,840
 Tony was therefore able to really sort of back up a pickup truck at the back of the

287
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:11,400
 exchange and take away all of those components and dismantle all of those components.

288
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:16,480
 And they were absolutely perfect and it was just that time he really couldn't have been

289
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:17,760
 better.

290
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:22,080
 So Alan Turing actually left Bletchley Park after about three and a half years.

291
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:28,160
 His involvement really finished, he went to the United States, Turing was not actually

292
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:30,760
 involved in the development of Colossus.

293
00:22:30,760 --> 00:22:36,360
 Some of the statistical work that he did was used by the people, predominantly Bill Tut,

294
00:22:36,360 --> 00:22:41,040
 who's the guy that actually reconstructed the Lorenz machine, quite remarkably a remarkable

295
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:44,760
 feat, indeed.

296
00:22:44,760 --> 00:22:47,880
 Turing got involved in things like speech scrambling systems and went to the United

297
00:22:47,880 --> 00:22:52,360
 States and was involved in other projects, so he wasn't really involved in Colossus.

298
00:22:52,360 --> 00:22:56,120
 At the end of the war, Turing went to work for the National Physics Laboratory, again

299
00:22:56,120 --> 00:23:00,760
 pursuing these sort of ideas, and then of course he ended up at the University of Manchester,

300
00:23:00,760 --> 00:23:06,000
 where Max Newman, who had run the department where the Colossus computers had been located

301
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,600
 at Bletchley Park, also ended up as the head of mathematics and obviously then at that

302
00:23:10,600 --> 00:23:16,160
 point Turing became involved in the very early computer developments in Britain.

303
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:20,760
 Gordon Welchman immigrated to the United States at the end of the war, in 1948 he immigrated

304
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:25,440
 to the United States and became involved in many of the early American computing developments,

305
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:31,080
 project World Wind, Welchman worked at MIT, taught the first course in computing science

306
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:32,080
 at MIT.

307
00:23:32,080 --> 00:23:37,680
 So Turing then was involved in things like Manchester Baby and the Franti Mark I and

308
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:42,820
 the early computing developments, I guess this is well known of course, Turing tragically

309
00:23:42,820 --> 00:23:46,480
 committed suicide in 1954.

310
00:23:55,440 --> 00:23:58,360
 [Music]

